{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And HaShem spoke.</b> The reason for the expression of אמירה (saying) is that the warning comes with force — that if he enters he will die — as Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah expounded (Torat Kohanim here). Therefore He informed him that this is nothing but extraordinary loving devotion, like a physician who strives to rouse the patient so that he not die — as in the parable of Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah — and this is what He alluded to by the expression of אמירה." "<b>Or</b> it may intend that HaShem exalted and elevated Moses, so that even speech pertaining to Aaron alone — HaShem did not speak with Aaron, but with Moses. This is the meaning of <b>and HaShem said to Moses</b> — in the manner of ״וַה׳ הֶאֱמִירְךָ״ (Deuteronomy 26:18). And you will find that they said, of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 14:21; beginning of Torat Kohanim), that even where Scripture says ״and HaShem spoke to Moses and to Aaron,״ the intent is to Moses that he should tell Aaron. And this is your sign: even the commandments of Aaron were not said to Aaron, for if HaShem had been speaking with Aaron He would not have needed to command him through an emissary." "<b>And he shall not come</b> — it said the letter vav at the beginning of the utterance. Beyond what we have written above, it further appears possible to say that since Moses had previously come and said to Aaron on the first day of his anointment (Leviticus 9:7) ״קְרַב אֶל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ וַעֲשֵׂה״ etc., the implication of those words indicated that he should approach the altar but not to the area beyond the altar — and the illuminators of the world, who are our Rabbis of blessed memory (Pesachim 41a), stated that a negative prohibition that emerges from the implication of a positive command is itself a positive command — and consequently there had already preceded for him a positive command regarding entry. Therefore He added here a negative prohibition concerning the matter and said <b>and he shall not come.</b>" "<b>At all times</b> — but there is a time when he may come, which is what He said afterward: ״בְּזֹאת יָבֹא״ etc. — once a year. And the reason He preceded <b>and he shall not come at all times</b> before the commandment of his entry once a year, is to say: if he does not come at all times, then he will merit to come at that time which I am commanding you; and if he comes at another time, he will not come at all times — even at the time of permission. And examine closely His saying <b>at all times</b>: our Rabbis of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim) expounded that this comes to prohibit even on Yom Kippur itself — he may not enter except for those things written in the true record, to burn incense and to sprinkle. And according to this approach we have found room for another precise reading in the additional vav of <b>and he shall not come</b> — to say: beyond the warning regarding the days of the year, it further adds to say that even on Yom Kippur itself he shall not come. And the warning regarding the rest of the days, our Rabbis of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim) expounded from the verse itself, which said ״אֶל הַקֹּדֶשׁ״ and did not suffice to say ״מִבֵּית לַפָּרֹכֶת״ — and the place He is commanding him about is self-understood. And let it not trouble you that the vav is prefixed, for such is the way of Scripture, and in other places I have elaborated in demonstrating the matter." "<b>For in the cloud I will appear.</b> — meaning, since Scripture had previously said (Leviticus 9:23) ״וַיֵּרָא כְבוֹד ה׳,״ He informed him that this quality alone is not what is found within [the Holy of Holies], but rather a great quality — a supernal light — for גָּבוֹהַּ מֵעַל גָּבוֹהַּ שׁוֹמֵר (high above high, a watchman watches). And this is His saying <b>I will appear upon the Ark-cover.</b>"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:27.884055507Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}